<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.18.2: Identifying Domain for a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01189</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.18.2: Identifying Domain for a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>43b50209-567b-428f-a1e0-5c043274b51d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A Sierpinski triangle can be created by starting with an equilateral
        triangle, breaking the triangle into 4 congruent equilateral triangles, and then removing
        the middle triangle. Starting from a single black equilateral triangle:</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <media
          alt="Four triangles show the stages of creating a Sierpinski triangle: the first is solid black, and each subsequent triangle has progressively smaller white triangles cut out, forming a fractal pattern.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_21_03_01.svg" />
        </media>

      </para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">1. Let <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>S</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> be the number of black
            triangles in Step <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. Define <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>S</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            recursively. </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>S</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>S</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mi>S</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> for <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo>≥</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">2. Andre and Lin are asked to write an equation for <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>S</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> that isn’t recursive.
            Andre writes <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>S</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            for <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo>≥</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, while Lin writes <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>S</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> for <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo>≥</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. Whose equation do you
            think is correct? Be prepared to show your reasoning. </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">They are both correct. For example: Lin is correct because if the
            image shows Steps 1 to 4, her equation gives the correct number of triangles for each
            step. Andre is correct since if we start with the black triangle, which is like Step 0,
            then Step 1 is the one with 3 triangles and Step 2 has 9 triangles, and those numbers
            match his equation. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Learning About Sierpinski’s Triangle</title>
      <para id="para-00009">Watch the following video to learn more about how to write a pattern as
        an nth term.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4"
          src="https://openstax.org/r/sierpinski-triangle" />
      </media>
    </section>
    <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
    will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->

    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <!--Start
        interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
        have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
        button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00010">Here is a geometric sequence. Find the missing terms.</para>
            <para id="para-00011">3, ____, 6, ____, 12, ____, 24</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00012">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00013"><m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:msqrt>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msqrt>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              , <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  <m:msqrt>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msqrt>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              , <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                  <m:msqrt>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msqrt>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              or equivalent</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
